WEBVTT

00:32.460 --> 00:34.530
Hello, Grand Prix.

00:34.530 --> 00:36.800
Hello? Hello?

00:36.800 --> 00:41.370
“M. J.”: “Mighty Jack,” a 1968-69
Tsuburaya Productions TV series.

00:36.800 --> 00:41.370
Um… about the “M. J.” T-shirts…

00:41.370 --> 00:43.230
What? “M. J.?”

00:43.230 --> 00:48.980
Mr. Okada said that I can print
”M. J.” T-shirts, and…

00:48.980 --> 00:53.320
Look. It’s the New Year’s vacation.
Nobody’s here. Call back later.

00:53.320 --> 00:55.910
Uh, then…

00:58.490 --> 01:02.320
<i>Ugh! What’s with these idiots, calling</i>
<i>up right after the New Year?</i>

01:02.320 --> 01:04.600
Damn, I hate otaku!

01:16.080 --> 01:21.270
<b>Otaku no Video</b>

01:21.930 --> 01:27.730
<i>Over the endless wasteland,
I run alone for all I am worth…</i>

01:27.730 --> 01:34.420
<i>Embracing the hope of an</i>
<i>unseen world far away.</i>

01:34.420 --> 01:40.160
<i>The only thing I believe in
is glowing passion.</i>

01:40.160 --> 01:43.040
<i>I will be a raging inferno!</i>

01:43.040 --> 01:54.440
<i>No one will be able to</i>
<i>stop my heart’s beat!</i>

01:54.440 --> 02:00.550
<i>One of these days, I will find</i>
<i>out the truth of love.</i>

02:00.550 --> 02:05.930
<i>Everything in this world</i>
<i>will be mine.</i>

02:05.930 --> 02:11.420
<i>We’re bound together by friendship,</i>
<i>it’s a thick bond.</i>

02:11.420 --> 02:14.440
<i>Don’t get in my way!</i>
<i>(Don’t get in my way!)</i>

02:14.440 --> 02:17.260
<i>I will follow my path!</i>
<i>(I will follow my path!)</i>

02:17.260 --> 02:23.680
<i>Fight! (Fight!) Fight! (Fight!)</i>
<i>Fight! (Fight!) Fight! (Fight!)</i>

02:23.680 --> 02:29.080
<i>Fight, Otaking!</i>

03:02.820 --> 03:07.040
Hello? Is this the Kubo residence?
This is Yoko calling.

03:07.040 --> 03:10.150
Oh, good morning.

03:10.150 --> 03:13.000
Huh? Tennis practice?

03:13.590 --> 03:15.960
Sure, okay, got it.

03:15.960 --> 03:17.860
Okay, then I’ll meet you at the courts.

03:17.860 --> 03:21.700
Sure… See you!

03:23.600 --> 03:25.470
All right! Let’s go!

03:29.680 --> 03:32.560
Are you ready? Here it comes!

03:41.590 --> 03:44.070
Now’s my chance!

03:53.070 --> 03:55.260
Yes! I did it!

03:55.260 --> 03:56.800
Oh, c’mon…

03:56.800 --> 04:01.590
Gosh, you didn’t have to
hit it so hard, you know!

04:02.010 --> 04:06.740
You have take practice seriously,
otherwise it won’t do you any good!

04:06.740 --> 04:08.280
Just forget it!

04:11.220 --> 04:15.020
You’ve got to be nicer to girls if
you want to go out with them!

04:19.690 --> 04:20.860
Jeez…

04:20.860 --> 04:22.740
Hey, Kubo!

04:22.740 --> 04:27.330
If you don’t hit the showers soon,
we’re gonna leave without you!

04:27.330 --> 04:29.160
Just kidding!

04:32.310 --> 04:34.540
What a stale joke.

04:59.330 --> 05:00.780
He’s a surfer, right?

05:00.780 --> 05:02.830
Maybe he’s around Radio City.

05:02.830 --> 05:03.870
Huh?

05:03.870 --> 05:06.700
You know what Hamaguchi is tooling
around in these days?

05:06.700 --> 05:07.900
A Porsche!

05:07.900 --> 05:10.040
Uncool!

05:10.040 --> 05:12.270
Saabs are the “in” thing now!

05:12.270 --> 05:13.970
So you’ve got one, right?

05:13.970 --> 05:16.870
Not yet, but I will someday!

05:16.870 --> 05:20.550
Who knows when that will be?

05:20.550 --> 05:24.220
Hey, Yoshida, isn’t that a
Boathouse sweatshirt?

05:24.220 --> 05:25.710
I stood in line four hours for it!

05:25.710 --> 05:28.250
You’ve got guts!

05:30.500 --> 05:32.560
Thanks for the food.

05:32.560 --> 05:33.640
Uh-huh…

05:34.700 --> 05:35.830
Where to next?

05:35.830 --> 05:38.770
I haven’t had enough to drink yet.

05:38.770 --> 05:42.080
Have you heard about the new joint
that just opened near here?

05:42.080 --> 05:44.740
Hey… you going home already, Kubo?

05:45.050 --> 05:48.450
Uh, yeah. There’s a match tomorrow.

05:50.480 --> 05:51.990
Well, catch you later.

05:53.620 --> 05:57.160
He’s the only guy we know who
takes the tennis club seriously.

06:02.430 --> 06:05.380
Miyoshi, do a Gundam parody
again with me, won’t ya?

06:05.380 --> 06:07.340
But it’s a little difficult without the materials…

06:07.340 --> 06:08.530
“Switch on!”

06:08.530 --> 06:11.570
Oh, wait! Tanaka hasn’t
caught up with us yet.

06:11.570 --> 06:13.430
What a slowpoke.

06:13.430 --> 06:17.370
Guess what I got! A Harlock sticker!

06:17.370 --> 06:20.380
Huh? Really? Lemme see!

06:21.650 --> 06:24.280
“Circle”: a (typically) small fan club.

06:21.650 --> 06:24.280
My friend’s making them in his circle!

06:24.280 --> 06:28.020
Eh, really? That’s news to me.

06:28.960 --> 06:32.470
“One does not care to acknowledge
the mistakes of one’s youth.”

06:33.890 --> 06:36.910
“Comiket”: “Comic Market”, a fair
of self-published works (“doujinshi”).

06:33.890 --> 06:36.910
Still, could you believe how packed
the last Comiket was?

06:36.910 --> 06:40.580
True, true… Wall-to-wall clueless,
know-nothing newbies!

06:40.580 --> 06:44.540
Yeah, like that ditz in the
Minky Momo costume!

06:44.540 --> 06:47.800
Hey, folks, ‘scuse me, sorry…

06:47.800 --> 06:51.640
Captain! All crew members
present and accounted for.

06:51.640 --> 06:53.470
Very well! Launch!

06:53.470 --> 06:57.850
Roger! Auxiliary engines engaged!

06:58.420 --> 07:01.690
Huh? Kubo? Well, if it isn’t Kubo!

07:03.270 --> 07:06.520
Eh? Tanaka! I haven’t
seen you in ages!

07:06.520 --> 07:08.130
Not since high school!

07:08.130 --> 07:10.630
Yeah! What are you up to these days?

07:10.630 --> 07:14.370
I’m in the Manga Research Group
and the Jack-of-All-Trades circle.

07:14.370 --> 07:15.730
Huh?

07:15.730 --> 07:17.390
Say…

07:17.390 --> 07:21.960
…art thou a friend of
our liege Tanaka?

07:21.960 --> 07:24.130
Huh?

07:25.880 --> 07:33.410
1982/03/18:
Broadcast premiere of
”Magical Princess Minky Momo.”

07:34.310 --> 07:40.550
<b>Portrait of an Otaku</b>

07:56.340 --> 08:05.750
Tamatani Junichi
(pseudonym)
Born 1963.
Employed in the
sales division
of a machining
tools company.
Years as an otaku: 13.

08:05.750 --> 08:07.950
What kind of circles did you
participate in during your university days?

08:09.780 --> 08:13.890
Well, I was in a Science Fiction
Research Group, and others like that.

08:13.460 --> 08:20.800
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

08:13.890 --> 08:18.050
We published some fanzines…

08:18.050 --> 08:20.800
…and watched anime together.

08:22.090 --> 08:24.790
Uh… we were even working
on plans for our own film.

08:25.390 --> 08:31.430
Plans? Yeah, well, we had
a lot of plans for it…

08:31.430 --> 08:35.040
…but due to one thing or another,
nothing much came of them.

08:35.040 --> 08:38.700
I think it would have been a hell of
a flick, if we’d managed to do it.

08:40.070 --> 08:42.610
Fanzines?

08:42.610 --> 08:46.120
Well, we published three
fanzines, all told.

08:46.120 --> 08:48.330
Well, first…

08:48.330 --> 08:54.190
…we put out a photocopied
”Issue No. 2” first.

08:54.190 --> 08:56.390
It was a hit…

08:56.390 --> 08:59.950
…and we sold about 150 copies.

08:59.950 --> 09:03.930
We sold them at Comiket…

09:03.930 --> 09:08.310
…which at that time wasn’t so big.
Still, they were pretty popular.

09:08.310 --> 09:10.830
And then…

09:16.780 --> 09:21.080
And then, we made a live-action
special effects fanzine as well.

09:21.080 --> 09:24.880
We used offset printing
for the very first time.

09:24.880 --> 09:28.320
It was really beautiful,
that first time…

09:28.320 --> 09:34.860
We printed over 300 copies, and
went around selling them all.

09:34.860 --> 09:42.090
Those 300 copies we sold were
a record for our SF club.

09:42.090 --> 09:44.180
Any other activities?

09:48.580 --> 09:53.280
Well, let’s see, we did research
on manga, animation…

09:53.280 --> 09:56.310
…that and competitive
mah jongg research…

09:56.310 --> 09:59.820
Let me see…
”Lolita complex” studies, and…

09:59.820 --> 10:04.890
…there was an “S. F.” club inside
our SF club, and that was for…

10:04.890 --> 10:07.990
“Sailor Fuku”: Japanese schoolgirl
sailor suit uniforms.

10:04.890 --> 10:07.990
…research on “Sailor Fuku…”

10:07.990 --> 10:10.990
Let me see… what else…

10:10.990 --> 10:15.970
…oh, yeah, there was a group for Toho
special effects, like Godzilla.

10:15.970 --> 10:24.510
So each member of the SF Group had
his own particular thing to do.

10:26.790 --> 10:31.180
And collectively we called it a
”Science Fiction Research Group.”

10:31.180 --> 10:33.020
Me?

10:33.020 --> 10:37.600
Well, I was a member of all
the groups, of course.

10:37.600 --> 10:41.560
I remember the good old days,
the hot saké-fueled debates…

10:41.560 --> 10:45.860
…declarations like
”sailor suits shall rule!”

10:45.860 --> 10:49.410
Thinking about it makes
me feel very nostalgic.

10:49.740 --> 10:51.580
Did you have any real friends?

11:08.760 --> 11:11.600
What right do you have to ask
me something like that?

11:11.600 --> 11:12.890
I had a lot of friends…

11:12.890 --> 11:18.130
…to watch anime and go
to Comiket together.

11:18.130 --> 11:23.270
Well… I don’t know if I can call
them my “real friends,” but…

11:23.270 --> 11:28.470
…we’d stay up all night
drinking… and…

11:28.470 --> 11:33.450
…discussing the significance of the
Macross Valkyrie transformation.

11:33.450 --> 11:36.420
Then we’d catch the 4 AM train
from Osaka to Nagoya…

11:36.420 --> 11:39.130
…and go to the Nagoya Komika.

11:39.130 --> 11:45.190
Oh, Komika is Nagoya’s
spot sale fanzine market…

11:45.190 --> 11:47.810
I had tons of friends to do
things like that with!

11:47.810 --> 11:49.840
What?

11:49.840 --> 11:51.530
Now?

11:51.530 --> 11:56.010
Well, even nowadays…
some of my juniors in the club…

11:56.010 --> 11:59.480
…still visit me occasionally.

12:02.980 --> 12:06.360
I’m pretty glad that I was once
a part of the SF club.

12:06.920 --> 12:11.220
Huh? Well, uh, I mean, I had
the best time of my life…

12:11.220 --> 12:17.060
…when I belonged to the
SF club, in university.

12:17.060 --> 12:19.920
It was very fulfilling.

12:19.920 --> 12:21.620
When did you graduate?

12:27.130 --> 12:29.900
Actually, I dropped out.

12:30.810 --> 12:33.550
The most popular “research groups”
are those devoted to science fiction…

12:33.550 --> 12:39.060
…followed by those for manga,
anime and mah jongg.

12:39.060 --> 12:42.950
However, under the umbrella of an
SF club, “research” is done on…

12:42.950 --> 12:46.680
…anime, manga, the
”Lolita complex,” sailor suits…

12:46.680 --> 12:48.730
…perverted matters, and
countless other subjects.

12:48.730 --> 12:50.570
These groups might be called
the forerunners…

12:50.570 --> 12:52.400
…of the “Jack-of-All-Trades” club.

13:13.440 --> 13:15.200
How’s university?

13:15.200 --> 13:17.820
Hmm? “How?”

13:17.820 --> 13:20.980
I just started! How am
I supposed to know?

13:21.520 --> 13:24.350
Oh yeah! I bumped into
Tanaka the other day!

13:24.350 --> 13:25.490
Tanaka?

13:25.490 --> 13:28.350
Yeah, he says he’s drawing
manga these days.

13:28.350 --> 13:30.750
Oh…

13:30.750 --> 13:33.210
So… are you still playing tennis?

13:33.210 --> 13:35.420
You bet. I’m giving it all I’ve got!

13:35.420 --> 13:36.760
That’s great!

13:36.760 --> 13:39.890
Now that I’m a second year, my time’s
all taken up with on-site training.

13:39.890 --> 13:43.230
Really? I guess you’re pretty busy.

13:43.230 --> 13:45.260
No, not really.

13:45.560 --> 13:48.740
Well, then, why don’t you come with
me to the May Fest at my school?

13:48.740 --> 13:50.900
My club is running a booth.

13:50.900 --> 13:52.310
Won’t you be too busy?

13:52.310 --> 13:53.520
Don’t sweat it!

13:53.520 --> 13:56.270
I’ll show you around campus!

14:11.920 --> 14:15.580
Delicious! Get your finger-lickin’
good yakisoba right here…

14:15.880 --> 14:17.710
Jeez! How can I sell anything…

14:17.710 --> 14:20.780
…in a place like this?

14:25.060 --> 14:27.810
Yo! Time’s up, my turn!

14:27.810 --> 14:29.750
Okay…

14:29.750 --> 14:34.850
Man, if we had a better location,
we’d be raking it in!

14:38.460 --> 14:40.230
Hey, Kubo!

14:41.530 --> 14:43.200
What the?!

14:43.200 --> 14:44.430
Yo!

14:44.430 --> 14:46.360
Ta… Tanaka?!

14:46.360 --> 14:48.580
Why on Earth are you
dressed up like that?

14:48.580 --> 14:50.710
And what are you doing
here, at my school?

14:50.710 --> 14:53.570
We made a deal so we could
sell our fanzine here.

14:53.570 --> 14:55.770
The finest: “Matilda Ajan!”

14:55.770 --> 14:57.170
Buy one, pal!

14:57.170 --> 14:58.300
A fanzine?

14:58.300 --> 14:59.920
Yep.

14:59.920 --> 15:03.080
So you’ve got to dress like that
to sell your magazines?

15:03.080 --> 15:04.280
It’s not like that…

15:04.280 --> 15:06.260
Hey, Tanaka!

15:06.260 --> 15:09.890
Our “Artesia” sold
out this morning!

15:09.890 --> 15:10.750
What?

15:10.750 --> 15:14.470
Are you serious?! I’m jealous!

15:14.770 --> 15:19.370
Man! Our ‘zines aren’t
selling at all!

15:20.280 --> 15:22.900
Err… what’s with her?

15:22.900 --> 15:25.570
Eh? Who, Sato?

15:25.570 --> 15:28.330
You met her the other
day, didn’t you?

15:28.580 --> 15:30.250
How do you do?

15:30.250 --> 15:34.960
But… this isn’t what
she looked like before.

15:34.960 --> 15:37.710
Of course not! She’s cosplaying!

15:37.710 --> 15:38.940
Yup.

15:38.940 --> 15:42.130
Co… Cosplay? What’s that?

15:42.130 --> 15:44.890
What? You don’t know
what “cosplay” is?

15:44.890 --> 15:48.560
It’s when you make a costume of your
favorite character and wear it.

15:48.560 --> 15:50.600
It’s really fun!

15:50.600 --> 15:53.120
A costume, you say…

15:56.870 --> 15:59.770
Does she go to school here?

15:59.770 --> 16:02.380
It doesn’t matter, does it?

16:02.380 --> 16:05.280
Just buy one of our fanzines, okay?

16:05.280 --> 16:10.080
I’ll even throw in a set of Zambot 3
blueprints as a bonus.

16:11.320 --> 16:13.750
Hey, wilt thou buy one or not?

16:13.750 --> 16:16.540
“Ah, what a delightful youth.”

16:16.540 --> 16:18.700
Our club’s “she-male” fanzine
is really good!

16:18.700 --> 16:22.420
“During the next 30 minutes your
soul will leave your body…

16:22.420 --> 16:26.490
…and will enter this mysterious
dimension.”

16:30.530 --> 16:37.870
1982/05/01:
British troops invade
the Falkland Islands.

16:39.010 --> 16:44.810
<b>Portrait of an Otaku</b>

16:59.660 --> 17:08.710
lkuta Yuudai
(pseudonym)
Born 1969.
Computer
programmer.
Years as an otaku: 9.

17:08.920 --> 17:11.420
What kind of work
do you do now?

17:12.310 --> 17:16.510
I’m a computer programmer.

17:14.040 --> 17:23.350
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

17:16.510 --> 17:18.510
So far, I’ve worked on writing…

17:18.510 --> 17:24.200
…relational databases and graphic
accounting software.

17:24.200 --> 17:25.850
I attended Waseda University.

17:25.850 --> 17:29.750
Back then, I was into
mini communications…

17:29.750 --> 17:34.560
…and got to know lots of
people in mass media.

17:34.560 --> 17:35.890
So it was a lot of fun.

17:35.890 --> 17:37.160
Huh? Animation?

17:37.160 --> 17:39.890
Well, I used to watch it a bit…

17:39.890 --> 17:43.400
Yeah, and I did get interested
in fanzines, and…

17:43.400 --> 17:45.940
…and I even went to Comiket.

17:45.940 --> 17:48.870
But these days, I have a life.

17:48.870 --> 17:51.630
Were you in any circles
during university?

17:52.180 --> 17:55.040
I was in the Audio club.

17:55.040 --> 17:59.860
Like, buying parts to make
amps by ourselves…

17:59.860 --> 18:02.650
…working day and night researching
which components…

18:02.650 --> 18:06.190
…which amps, which players…

18:06.190 --> 18:11.100
…would make the ideal system.

18:11.100 --> 18:14.330
The best speakers by far
have got to be Diatones.

18:14.330 --> 18:16.440
Though Yamaha’s are…

18:16.440 --> 18:18.100
Oh, thank you.

18:16.600 --> 18:18.100
Here you are.

18:19.640 --> 18:23.030
To tell you the truth, I actually
liked computers more.

18:23.030 --> 18:27.610
But even in the Audio club, some
of us were into computers, too.

18:28.780 --> 18:31.670
We wrote software…

18:31.670 --> 18:36.780
…and bought parts
to make boards…

18:36.780 --> 18:38.630
…and stuff like that.

18:38.630 --> 18:41.990
Well, I only touched
a little on anime.

18:41.990 --> 18:46.680
My main interests lay with audio
equipment and computers.

18:46.680 --> 18:49.190
Have you ever worn costumes?

18:49.810 --> 18:51.220
I beg your pardon?!

18:51.220 --> 18:53.060
Like I said before…

18:53.060 --> 18:56.500
…I was only into anime a little bit!

18:59.480 --> 19:03.150
That has nothing to do with this!

19:04.450 --> 19:09.210
It’s not like I’m ashamed
of my past or anything.

19:10.830 --> 19:13.340
Are you familiar with this fanzine?

19:13.340 --> 19:16.710
Where did you find this?! Jeez…

19:16.710 --> 19:18.220
What about this photo?

19:18.220 --> 19:23.000
What’s that?! Look, I’m a
normal human being too.

19:23.000 --> 19:24.840
Besides, there’s nothing wrong
with cosplay, is there?

19:24.840 --> 19:27.100
Besides, it was a long time
ago. What’s the big deal?

19:27.100 --> 19:29.580
Is it hot in here?

19:33.920 --> 19:37.140
People have things in their pasts
that they don’t like to talk about.

19:37.140 --> 19:41.220
As Major Char, the Red Comet, said:

19:42.260 --> 19:47.410
“One does not care to acknowledge
the mistakes of one’s youth.”

19:52.200 --> 19:55.530
Surprisingly, the ratio of people
who did cosplay…

19:55.530 --> 19:58.270
…during their otaku days
isn’t high at all.

19:58.270 --> 20:01.100
No more than 15%.

20:01.610 --> 20:05.050
However, the otaku who
did indulge in cosplay…

20:05.050 --> 20:10.550
…tended to be repeat offenders,
as this chart clearly shows.

20:10.550 --> 20:15.850
Think of them as similar to those
who repeatedly visit Disneyland.

20:15.850 --> 20:17.890
Hooked by the indescribable
charm of cosplay…

20:17.890 --> 20:20.660
…the otaku find themselves
addicted to it.

20:41.040 --> 20:43.370
I’m sorry I can’t make it.

20:43.370 --> 20:45.550
A friend called me at the
last minute and…

20:45.950 --> 20:48.970
It’s okay. It wasn’t
that important.

20:48.970 --> 20:51.310
Liar! You’re mad
at me, aren’t you?

20:51.310 --> 20:53.170
No, I’m not.

20:53.170 --> 20:55.920
Well, okay, if you say so.

20:55.920 --> 20:57.120
By the way…

20:57.120 --> 20:58.050
What?

20:58.050 --> 21:01.560
I’ve… been thinking about
quitting the Tennis club.

21:01.560 --> 21:03.660
Why?

21:03.660 --> 21:05.850
I don’t know.

21:05.850 --> 21:07.810
I can’t seem to get into
it these days.

21:07.810 --> 21:11.810
Oh, I know! You’re in
the freshman slump.

21:11.810 --> 21:15.570
No, it’s nothing like that…

21:15.570 --> 21:19.280
It’s just that I can’t seem
to get excited about it.

21:20.180 --> 21:21.680
You’re weird, Kubo.

21:21.680 --> 21:23.240
Am I?

21:23.240 --> 21:26.910
But it’s one of the things
I like about you.

21:26.910 --> 21:32.250
I envy people who can focus
their minds on something.

21:32.250 --> 21:33.530
Think so?

21:33.530 --> 21:34.420
Yup!

21:34.420 --> 21:38.090
Oh, that reminds me! Would you like to
go see a movie with me this Sunday?

21:38.090 --> 21:41.630
There’s a “Kramer vs. Kramer”
revival going on now.

21:49.850 --> 21:55.100
Wow! From the looks of this place,
your life revolves around manga!

21:55.410 --> 21:59.210
I’m no stud like you are, so it’s not
like girls will be coming over.

21:59.210 --> 22:01.810
So I can be pretty laid back.

22:04.740 --> 22:08.800
By the way, are you still going
out with Ueno Yoshiko?

22:08.800 --> 22:11.960
Well… Not so much these days.

22:11.960 --> 22:14.900
I bet she’s gotten real pretty.

22:14.900 --> 22:17.800
Yeah. She really looks like
a mature woman.

22:17.800 --> 22:23.210
You’ve got it good, really!
Just going on with a normal life!

22:23.210 --> 22:24.510
It’s not like that.

22:24.510 --> 22:27.800
We can’t act like we’re still in high
school anymore, you know.

22:27.800 --> 22:30.770
Ah… That school festival sure was fun!

22:30.770 --> 22:34.830
Yeah! Those were the days!

22:35.460 --> 22:36.790
You think so too?

22:36.790 --> 22:38.110
Sure.

22:38.110 --> 22:40.650
I wish I could go back…

22:40.650 --> 22:41.560
Okay!

22:41.560 --> 22:44.460
I’m gonna take you to a neat place!

22:45.660 --> 22:46.800
Where?

22:46.800 --> 22:51.040
A place where every day
is like a school festival!

22:51.400 --> 22:52.800
Bang!

22:53.080 --> 22:55.160
Target scope open!

22:55.160 --> 22:57.440
Raise video targeting sight
twenty degrees!

22:57.440 --> 22:59.910
Disengage safety lock!

23:04.550 --> 23:05.680
Welcome, comrade.

23:05.680 --> 23:08.220
This is our homeland!

23:14.560 --> 23:16.080
Did Mr. Tsurumaki pull
another all-nighter?

23:16.480 --> 23:17.120
Yeah.

23:17.120 --> 23:18.580
Hey, Kubo!

23:18.980 --> 23:21.080
What’s the matter? Come on in!

23:44.390 --> 23:46.960
Ah, Tanaka. Who have you got here?

23:46.960 --> 23:48.550
Let me introduce you.

23:48.550 --> 23:49.340
This is Mr. Hino.

23:49.340 --> 23:50.180
Hello.

23:50.180 --> 23:52.600
He’s our special effects and
science fiction specialist.

23:52.600 --> 23:54.200
Nice to meet you.

23:54.200 --> 23:56.200
The guy in the corner with
the flat-top is Mr. Miyoshi.

23:56.600 --> 23:57.520
Hello.

23:57.520 --> 23:59.100
A pro-wrestling and
idol specialist…

23:59.100 --> 24:02.360
…he knows techniques that could down
a yakuza in ten seconds flat.

24:02.360 --> 24:04.040
The girl next to him is Miss Honda.

24:03.270 --> 24:04.040
Here.

24:04.040 --> 24:06.080
She’s an expert on pretty boys
and alcoholic beverages.

24:06.080 --> 24:07.340
And then there’s Mr. Iiyama…

24:07.340 --> 24:08.970
…a military and games expert.

24:08.970 --> 24:10.820
Nice to meet you.

24:11.290 --> 24:15.790
And the guy sleeping in the doorway
is Mr. Tsurumaki, our night owl.

24:15.790 --> 24:21.350
His nocturnal habits allow our circle
to operate twenty-four hours a day.

24:21.350 --> 24:24.790
Add me, the anime specialist…

24:24.790 --> 24:27.920
…and Sato, our brilliant
illustrator, and…

24:27.920 --> 24:30.300
…ten years from now…

24:30.300 --> 24:33.070
…the industry will be ours!

24:35.910 --> 24:37.530
So? Who’s he?

24:37.530 --> 24:40.390
Huh? Oh, yeah.
Let me introduce him.

24:40.390 --> 24:43.590
This is Kubo, the guy we met the other
day, in the elevator after the party.

24:43.590 --> 24:44.610
Hello.

24:44.610 --> 24:46.750
Oh, the one who plays tennis!

24:46.750 --> 24:47.820
Tennis?

24:47.820 --> 24:50.730
What’s a city boy like him doing here?

24:50.730 --> 24:53.390
“C-City boy?”

24:55.740 --> 25:04.230
1983/02/10:
Six 16-year-old boys commit
repeated assaults on homeless
persons in Yokohama,
killing 3 and injuring 13.

25:05.380 --> 25:10.900
<b>Portrait of an Otaku</b>

25:12.970 --> 25:17.910
This interview took place in May 1991.

25:22.120 --> 25:27.590
Harold Shioda
(pseudonym)
Born 1961.
Currently wandering
the USA.
Years as an otaku: 15.

25:24.860 --> 25:27.630
Oh, what a crock!
What was the staff thinking?

25:27.630 --> 25:31.030
That’s an incredibly large
collection of video tapes.

25:31.030 --> 25:35.150
Compared to others,
it’s not that big…

25:35.150 --> 25:37.840
I only collect the few
genres that I like.

25:37.840 --> 25:43.140
Let’s see, special effects, science
fiction, not too many movies…

25:39.160 --> 25:46.500
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

25:43.140 --> 25:46.500
…mostly animation and idol stars.

25:46.500 --> 25:50.440
I also have a few TV commercials…
Just some really rare ones.

25:50.440 --> 25:52.940
How many times do you watch
what you’ve recorded?

25:52.940 --> 25:55.180
Um… I don’t.

25:55.180 --> 26:00.680
You see, I just record them for
the sake of my collection.

26:00.680 --> 26:03.650
I don’t do it so I can watch them
again like other people do…

26:03.650 --> 26:07.560
I don’t do it for fun, or to analyze
the creators’ intentions.

26:07.560 --> 26:12.190
It’s for the collection… I just
want the perfect collection.

26:12.190 --> 26:14.060
When you record every
day like I do…

26:14.060 --> 26:16.900
…there’s always some new
anime coming out.

26:16.900 --> 26:19.630
So there simply isn’t
enough time…

26:19.630 --> 26:23.470
…for me to watch and
enjoy anything.

26:23.470 --> 26:26.380
What is the secret of building
up a good collection?

26:28.650 --> 26:31.880
Even though I have a
lot of tapes here…

26:31.880 --> 26:34.630
…not all of them were
recorded by me.

26:34.630 --> 26:39.220
Uh… by the time you get to my level,
you’re hooked into a vast network.

26:39.220 --> 26:43.660
From Hokkaido to Kyushu…
oh, even in Okinawa…

26:43.660 --> 26:48.000
…I’ve got friends like me, who are also
recording, right on schedule.

26:48.000 --> 26:50.000
They also give me a lot of
information quickly…

26:50.000 --> 26:52.070
…like what’s coming up locally…

26:52.070 --> 26:54.820
…so I can be ready for it.

26:54.820 --> 26:57.940
And those friends have friends…

26:57.940 --> 27:01.540
…in the local TV stations…
associates, that is…

27:01.540 --> 27:04.580
…so I can even find out which
commercials will play when.

27:04.580 --> 27:07.670
So I don’t miss anything.

27:07.670 --> 27:09.500
Actually, uh…

27:09.500 --> 27:13.750
…on stations in rural areas, it’s
easier to find good programs.

27:13.750 --> 27:15.930
They tend to run the more
uncommon shows and…

27:15.930 --> 27:21.860
…in their original form, too…

27:21.860 --> 27:24.660
…like, with the openings
and endings intact…

27:24.660 --> 27:26.930
Excuse me.

27:26.930 --> 27:28.540
Hello?

27:28.540 --> 27:30.910
Well, well, if it isn’t Mr. Tamura.

27:30.910 --> 27:32.000
I’ve been waiting for your call.

27:32.000 --> 27:34.280
Not to worry… I’m all set
here. “GodMars,” right?

27:34.280 --> 27:35.640
Ah! Hold on a sec!

27:35.640 --> 27:40.240
Rats! This is not good…
not good at all!

27:40.240 --> 27:43.450
Huh? No, I was waiting…

27:43.450 --> 27:46.060
…but the tape… hold on…

27:46.060 --> 27:47.660
Okay, okay, it’s all right!

27:47.660 --> 27:49.880
I won’t do a repeat of last time.

27:49.880 --> 27:52.630
Damn this thing.

27:52.630 --> 27:54.890
You would laugh at me…

27:54.890 --> 27:57.590
…for making such a basic
blunder again, right?

27:57.590 --> 28:00.890
It won’t come off! Come off!

28:00.890 --> 28:03.210
Oh, gimme a break!

28:03.210 --> 28:06.080
Eh? Nothing to worry about…

28:06.080 --> 28:09.450
Nothing to worry about… I won’t make
that mistake again… Here it comes…

28:09.450 --> 28:11.900
Hold on a sec… Just a sec…

28:11.900 --> 28:15.920
All set!

28:17.400 --> 28:19.960
I suppose this is it…

28:19.960 --> 28:22.710
Don’t worry. Don’t worry.
I’ll call you back when it’s over.

28:22.710 --> 28:24.740
Okay, okay. Talk to you later.

28:24.740 --> 28:26.580
Eh? Yes… Yes…
Yes! Yes!

28:26.580 --> 28:27.730
Yes… It’s starting.

28:27.730 --> 28:29.130
Yes, yes.

28:31.630 --> 28:40.690
Two weeks after this interview, he went to the America, in search of the ultimate videocassette collection.

28:47.490 --> 28:49.030
Look at this, Kubo!

28:52.450 --> 28:54.740
This is what we call “effects.”

28:54.740 --> 28:55.940
“Effects?”

28:55.940 --> 28:58.990
It’s using stuff like laser
beams and explosions.

28:58.990 --> 29:01.420
They’re all different, depending…

29:01.420 --> 29:04.300
…on the particular animator’s
style and habits.

29:04.300 --> 29:06.790
These guys are real
sticklers for detail.

29:06.790 --> 29:08.920
It’s not something just
anyone can do.

29:11.300 --> 29:14.350
The aerial battles in this
mecha are amazing!

29:14.350 --> 29:16.100
Look how realistic it is!

29:16.100 --> 29:19.380
He’s firing decoy heat
flares while jinking…

29:19.380 --> 29:22.400
…to avoid the guided missiles!

29:22.650 --> 29:23.820
Of all the key animators…

29:23.820 --> 29:27.010
…the man’s mecha sequences
are just terrific.

29:27.010 --> 29:30.990
Not only is the timing perfect,
but just look at that realism!

29:30.990 --> 29:34.480
It’s so cool!

29:34.480 --> 29:38.850
And that’s why this is really the cutting
edge of animation today!

29:38.850 --> 29:41.790
Wow! Now that I look
at them this way…

29:41.790 --> 29:44.970
…I see that different artists
have different styles.

29:44.970 --> 29:46.560
Naturally!

29:46.560 --> 29:48.640
And if you watch it
frame by frame…

29:48.640 --> 29:51.340
…you can see how the
images flow together.

29:51.340 --> 29:53.390
Yeah, it’s really interesting.

29:53.390 --> 29:55.070
It’s not all anime, you know.

29:55.070 --> 29:55.700
Huh?

29:55.700 --> 29:56.840
Here.

29:56.840 --> 29:58.650
Uh, thanks.

29:59.220 --> 30:02.650
The world of science-fiction and
special effects is also vast.

30:02.650 --> 30:05.380
Oh, is that so?

30:05.380 --> 30:10.070
Dost thou remember the
Shounen Drama Series on NHK?

30:10.070 --> 30:12.190
Yeah, I remember!

30:12.190 --> 30:13.790
I used to watch it every week.

30:13.790 --> 30:16.290
Would you like to watch it again?

30:16.290 --> 30:18.190
Well, sure, but…

30:18.190 --> 30:19.250
Kubo.

30:19.250 --> 30:23.210
Ta-da! I’ve got every episode,
right up to the end!

30:23.210 --> 30:26.470
Oh, wow, how did you get those?

30:27.380 --> 30:31.050
Like I said: There’s nothing
we can’t do.

30:31.050 --> 30:32.430
Wanna watch ‘em here?

30:32.430 --> 30:33.780
Uh, is it okay?

30:33.780 --> 30:38.020
Sure. You can even crash
here if you want, Kubo.

30:38.020 --> 30:39.690
Really?

30:40.790 --> 30:45.230
Of course, if you stay here once,
you can <i>never</i> get out.

30:45.230 --> 30:46.650
Huh?

30:57.370 --> 30:59.870
Hey, what’s the matter
with you these days?

30:59.870 --> 31:02.110
It seems like you’re never
home when I call.

31:02.110 --> 31:04.250
That’s not true.

31:04.250 --> 31:06.800
What are you doing until
so late at night?

31:06.800 --> 31:08.810
Is it a job?

31:08.810 --> 31:12.300
Sort of, but I don’t get paid for it.

31:12.300 --> 31:14.320
What kind of job is that?

31:14.320 --> 31:16.690
I’m helping Tanaka.

31:16.690 --> 31:18.830
Tanaka?

31:18.830 --> 31:20.760
Why aren’t you getting paid?

31:20.760 --> 31:22.830
Just what is it you’re doing?

31:22.830 --> 31:24.560
Oh, this and that.

31:24.560 --> 31:28.970
Besides, it’s not like I’m broke or
anything, and it’s kinda fun…

31:28.970 --> 31:31.780
…just like in high school.

31:31.780 --> 31:33.270
Kubo…

31:33.270 --> 31:37.160
I guess that for me, it’s like
playing make-believe.

31:38.580 --> 31:42.620
Listen to me. You can’t just
pretend to be a character.

31:42.620 --> 31:45.590
You’ve got to <i>become</i>
the character.

31:45.590 --> 31:47.050
Uh-huh.

31:47.050 --> 31:52.130
Watch me!
Eagle! Shark! Panther!

31:54.330 --> 31:56.900
Well?

31:58.540 --> 32:01.530
Ready? Now, these are
basic-level questions.

32:01.530 --> 32:03.020
Uh-huh…

32:03.020 --> 32:05.770
What country was the first
to cultivate the Triffid?

32:05.770 --> 32:08.980
How many crewmen
on the Enterprise?

32:08.980 --> 32:12.680
Who illustrated the cover of
SF Magazine’s premiere issue?

32:12.680 --> 32:14.720
Captain Future’s mother is?

32:14.720 --> 32:16.990
Where are the Super Giants from?

32:19.330 --> 32:20.550
I dunno…

32:20.550 --> 32:23.000
If you don’t know, then start studying.

32:25.710 --> 32:26.210
Uh, okay…

32:26.210 --> 32:28.700
Next question…

32:29.880 --> 32:32.400
Attention!

32:33.880 --> 32:40.020
Throughout the history of war, firearms
have been made for specific tasks.

32:40.020 --> 32:42.870
Take this riot shotgun.

32:42.870 --> 32:47.310
Its ability to kill at short
range is unmatched!

32:47.310 --> 32:50.070
The ignorant masses, with their
closed minds and prejudices…

32:50.070 --> 32:52.260
…don’t realize that a model gun
must strive to be a toy that is…

32:52.260 --> 32:55.050
…as close as possible to the real thing,
and yet, unable to cause injury.

32:55.050 --> 32:56.720
Such is the nature of a model gun!

32:56.720 --> 32:59.730
I don’t see airguns as model guns,
because they eject projectiles!

32:59.730 --> 33:02.250
But real guns shoot bullets, too.

33:03.650 --> 33:07.900
<i>Idiot</i>! We’re military fans,
not soldiers!

33:07.900 --> 33:10.460
Real guns are made to kill people!

33:11.170 --> 33:12.920
So that’s how it is…

33:23.250 --> 33:25.650
Oops, I went outside the lines.

33:25.650 --> 33:29.830
Oh, when that happens, let it dry, then you can scrape it off.

33:29.830 --> 33:32.110
Oh, really?

33:32.110 --> 33:35.070
Uh-huh. Oh, yeah…

33:35.070 --> 33:38.780
Here, here. Kubo, come
and try this on!

33:44.070 --> 33:46.240
Ah, I knew it would
look good on you.

33:46.240 --> 33:48.960
Look, Iiyama! He makes a great
Cobra, don’t you think?

33:49.650 --> 33:51.300
Yeah, pretty nifty.

33:51.300 --> 33:55.470
Kubo, go to the next Comiket
as Cobra. It fits you.

33:55.990 --> 33:57.800
Really? It fits me?

33:57.800 --> 34:02.520
Sure! You’re tall, and you look like you
just stepped off the drawing board!

34:02.520 --> 34:05.430
As for me, I’ll go as Dominique.
So, you’ll do it, right? Right?

34:05.430 --> 34:09.360
Really? Maybe I will!

34:11.300 --> 34:18.640
1983/09/01:
A Korean Airlines jet is shot
down by a Soviet plane.

34:20.330 --> 34:23.940
<b>Portrait of an Otaku</b>

34:26.400 --> 34:28.200
Watch out!

34:29.180 --> 34:37.300
Mamiya Kenji
(pseudonym)
Born 1969.
Girls’ school
English teacher.
Years as an otaku: 5.

34:49.580 --> 34:51.040
It sure is realistic, isn’t it?

34:51.790 --> 34:56.640
Well… we’ve certainly
souped the guns up.

34:54.210 --> 35:03.850
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

34:56.640 --> 34:58.800
So we need to wear proper
gear, like goggles…

34:58.800 --> 35:01.550
…’cause if you get hit in the
eye, kiss it goodbye.

35:01.550 --> 35:05.740
And you can break a tooth if
a shot hits it the wrong way.

35:05.740 --> 35:11.380
Usually, I wear a face mask, but since
it’s hot today, I took it off.

35:11.380 --> 35:14.820
Yeah… The gun’s been souped up.

35:14.820 --> 35:19.900
I can Swiss cheese an aluminum can…
pow-pow-pow… just like that.

35:21.530 --> 35:24.100
But I’ve heard that you’re
not supposed to do that.

35:24.100 --> 35:26.770
Have you ever thought of shooting
something that’s alive?

35:28.200 --> 35:30.100
Cats? I’m not sure about cats…

35:30.100 --> 35:34.210
…but I think I could easily kill
pigeons and small animals…

35:34.210 --> 35:36.960
…with a single shot.

35:39.590 --> 35:41.420
Do you like war?

35:43.090 --> 35:45.470
No… but…

35:45.470 --> 35:48.020
…like, wanting to shoot
someone else…

35:48.020 --> 35:52.150
…I haven’t really… I mean, no, I haven’t
thought about killing someone.

35:54.130 --> 36:00.150
When it comes down to it, I like the
beauty of the gun’s functionality.

36:01.370 --> 36:03.330
And what else…

36:03.330 --> 36:07.100
And of course, they look
cool, don’t you think?

36:07.100 --> 36:12.610
When facing a bigger opponent, if I’ve
got a gun, even though I’m small…

36:12.610 --> 36:14.830
…I can be on an equal footing.

36:14.830 --> 36:18.110
And this is small, but
it can kill people.

36:18.110 --> 36:24.380
Well, not really, but I like the
coolness of its utility…

36:24.380 --> 36:28.660
I don’t like war.

36:28.660 --> 36:30.800
I don’t want to kill people.

36:30.800 --> 36:36.310
I just enjoy playing this game, and…

36:36.310 --> 36:37.930
Quit slacking!

36:44.180 --> 36:48.410
Model gun and military otaku
are few in number.

36:48.410 --> 36:51.530
Now that the short-lived
survival game boom has passed…

36:51.530 --> 36:54.660
…only a small group of
connoisseurs remains.

36:54.660 --> 36:58.300
However, just as war will never
disappear from this world…

36:58.300 --> 37:00.400
…there will always be people
who are fascinated…

37:00.400 --> 37:03.250
…by weapons, the tools of killing.

37:04.230 --> 37:10.010
People who are attracted to “cool”
things will always be around.

37:10.010 --> 37:12.760
But here’s an interesting fact.

37:12.760 --> 37:18.000
We asked people who expressed a
fondness for model guns this question.

37:18.190 --> 37:32.870
Are you an anime fan?
I love it / I like it / I don’t really like it / I hate it

37:20.400 --> 37:26.200
That’s right. Anime fans do not share the
views of model gun and military fans.

37:26.200 --> 37:27.920
Could it be that the
reality-escaping anime fans…

37:27.920 --> 37:32.260
…and the super-realistic military
fans have nothing in common?

37:54.500 --> 37:57.800
Excuse me, can I take
a picture with you?

37:59.080 --> 37:59.940
With me?

37:59.940 --> 38:01.750
Here we go!

38:10.800 --> 38:15.640
Kubo! Kubo, are you listening to me?

38:16.260 --> 38:20.360
Huh? Yeah, I’m listening.

38:20.860 --> 38:24.230
Did you pull another all-nighter?

38:24.230 --> 38:26.070
Helping Tanaka?

38:26.470 --> 38:28.120
Xabungle…

38:28.120 --> 38:29.020
What?

38:29.020 --> 38:32.690
It’s a movie. People who went
to the premiere got cels.

38:32.690 --> 38:34.530
You stayed up all night for that?

38:34.530 --> 38:36.970
That’s right.

38:36.970 --> 38:39.540
Kubo, you’ve changed.

38:39.540 --> 38:41.880
Huh?

38:42.980 --> 38:45.710
Have you been putting on weight?

38:45.710 --> 38:48.950
Huh? Y… you think so?

38:51.470 --> 38:55.930
And you seem a little, well, sloppy.

38:55.930 --> 39:00.200
A… am I?

39:00.200 --> 39:04.390
Oh, yeah. We’re going to line
up again on the 16th.

39:04.390 --> 39:06.700
It’s “Unico”… Wanna
go see it with me?

39:06.700 --> 39:09.650
Uh… that’s all right.

39:09.650 --> 39:12.400
Oh…

39:12.400 --> 39:14.660
Oh, oh, yeah…
What day is it today?

39:15.020 --> 39:19.710
Huh? It’s the 11th… July 11th.

39:19.710 --> 39:22.630
Oh, no! Animage is already on sale!

39:23.490 --> 39:26.060
Huh?

39:28.330 --> 39:37.260
1983/10/13:
Court reaches verdict in the
Lockheed-Marubeni bribery scandal.
Ex-Prime Minister Kakuei Tanaka
is sentenced to 4 years in prison.

39:38.640 --> 39:44.970
<b>Portrait of an Otaku</b>

39:47.820 --> 39:56.880
Assistant recording
engineer.
Years as an otaku: 4.

39:47.820 --> 39:56.880
Mr. A
Born 1968.

40:04.280 --> 40:06.690
What do you do
for a living?

40:07.200 --> 40:16.050
My job? Well, I’m an assistant recording
engineer. I help make CDs and the like.

40:11.140 --> 40:18.610
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

40:18.800 --> 40:23.020
And in my line of work, you get to meet
idols, and lots of people like that.

40:23.020 --> 40:26.610
It’s got its perks.

40:26.610 --> 40:29.380
Since there’s a lot of technical
work involved…

40:29.380 --> 40:31.930
…I’ve gotten good with
the hardware.

40:31.930 --> 40:35.030
What? Oh, this?

40:35.030 --> 40:39.490
Well… I made it myself.

40:42.900 --> 40:50.310
It reduces the digital censoring
mosaic a bit.

40:50.310 --> 40:52.500
This kind of thing is easy to make.

40:52.500 --> 40:54.960
Do you have a girlfriend?

40:54.960 --> 40:59.380
Since my job is what it is and
all, I get a lot of nibbles…

40:59.380 --> 41:04.140
…but right now, I don’t have anyone…

41:04.140 --> 41:07.810
…who understands my hobbies.

41:07.810 --> 41:11.570
It’s not that I don’t like sex, but…

41:11.570 --> 41:14.090
When was your first experience?

41:14.570 --> 41:19.270
Huh? Well, uh, I’m still
a virgin, but so what?

41:20.880 --> 41:24.810
I mean, the two-dimensional world…

41:24.810 --> 41:31.550
…well, the screen can show things
that satisfy me plenty.

41:31.550 --> 41:34.460
I mean, isn’t that normal?

41:35.890 --> 41:39.290
With traces of feudal culture
still evident…

41:39.290 --> 41:43.630
…sexual roles and relationships
in modern Japan are in flux.

41:43.630 --> 41:46.600
However, in otaku society…

41:43.630 --> 41:50.270
“Were you a virgin when
you got married?”

41:46.600 --> 41:50.300
…the more traditional roles
of Japan still prevail…

41:50.300 --> 41:53.200
…as we can see from this graph.

41:53.200 --> 41:55.560
In fact, over 90% of the men and
women did not “know”…

41:55.560 --> 41:59.940
…another person until marriage.

41:59.940 --> 42:04.080
And Mr. A seems to be
one of these people.

42:05.100 --> 42:09.230
<b>March 1984</b>

42:06.930 --> 42:10.600
Poster: “Nausicaa of
the Valley of the Wind”

42:14.870 --> 42:18.470
Damn it!

42:18.470 --> 42:23.000
If we hadn’t had to go job hunting,
we wouldn’t be so far back in line!

42:23.000 --> 42:24.700
It can’t be helped, can it?

42:24.700 --> 42:26.440
By the way, where’s Tsurumaki?

42:27.080 --> 42:29.300
He’s trading cels over there.

42:29.300 --> 42:31.840
He’s always energetic at night.

42:31.840 --> 42:34.190
Yo, kids!

42:34.650 --> 42:37.830
What’cha lining up here for?

42:37.830 --> 42:40.320
We’re in line for a movie,
an anime movie.

42:40.320 --> 42:44.320
What?! You’re in line
to see a manga?!

42:44.320 --> 42:47.060
Why don’cha wait ‘til
it’s less crowded?

42:47.060 --> 42:49.460
Come on, let’s go…

42:49.460 --> 42:51.070
It’s not a manga! It’s anime!

42:51.070 --> 42:55.870
Same difference… but it seems
like it’s got a grip on you!

42:55.870 --> 42:57.550
Don’t you have anything
better to do?

42:57.550 --> 43:00.050
This hath nothing
to do with thee!

43:00.050 --> 43:04.250
What?! What is it with
you guys, anyway…

43:04.250 --> 43:06.340
…slinking around in the
shadows all the time…

43:06.340 --> 43:10.790
Hey, I said let’s go…
Okay? Okay?!

43:10.790 --> 43:13.860
Whaddya mean, “thee?”
Dumbass.

43:14.860 --> 43:16.710
What’s wrong with that idiot?

43:16.710 --> 43:19.460
Don’t bother. He’s just drunk.

43:19.460 --> 43:21.930
No! That’s not the problem!

43:21.930 --> 43:23.660
Hey, check it out!

43:23.660 --> 43:26.100
Look what I got my hands
on at the cel swap!

43:26.100 --> 43:28.750
The designs for the
Macross movie!

43:28.750 --> 43:30.250
What?!

43:32.480 --> 43:35.250
Damn! It’s really awesome!

43:35.920 --> 43:39.320
Looks like they’re all gonna
be Super Valkyries!

43:39.320 --> 43:43.350
And that’s not all! Anno’s Elint
Seekers are just too cool!

43:43.350 --> 43:45.860
Yeah… It’s so realistic!

43:45.860 --> 43:48.590
There’s a bubble canopy,
a heads-up display…

43:48.590 --> 43:51.070
…and a liquid-crystal target monitor!

43:51.070 --> 43:53.580
I hear that they’re already
using them at NASA!

43:53.580 --> 43:59.470
They say they’ll be using
laser weapons by 1990.

43:59.770 --> 44:02.460
Now that you mention it, it looks like
there’ll be a Super Valkyrie…

44:02.460 --> 44:07.010
…with a beam cannon mount. They’re
gonna call it a “Strike Valkyrie.”

44:07.010 --> 44:08.410
Here it is, look!

44:08.410 --> 44:12.110
I liked the female characters
better in the TV series.

44:12.110 --> 44:14.720
Doesn’t this Minmay look
like someone we know?

44:14.720 --> 44:16.520
Huh? Who?

44:16.520 --> 44:18.920
Yoshiko Ueno.

44:18.920 --> 44:22.690
What?! She doesn’t
look like this at all!

44:22.690 --> 44:25.450
Yes she does. She’s got an
expression just like this.

44:25.450 --> 44:29.800
Uh, well… you think so?

44:29.800 --> 44:33.410
Who did you say she
looks like? Me?

44:33.410 --> 44:35.010
Uh, no…

44:35.010 --> 44:37.330
You’re Misa, Sato!

44:37.330 --> 44:39.600
Yeah, that’s it! I think so, too!

44:39.600 --> 44:41.710
Kubo, why don’t you do
Hikaru next time…

44:41.710 --> 44:43.550
…and I’ll go as Misa!
Okay? Okay?

44:43.550 --> 44:45.010
Uh, okay…

44:45.010 --> 44:47.920
And why not Ueno as Minmay?

44:48.490 --> 44:50.590
W… what are you saying?!

44:50.590 --> 44:52.290
It’s a joke, a joke!

44:52.290 --> 44:55.060
Man…

45:02.980 --> 45:05.740
<i>I wonder what Ueno is up to.</i>

45:23.280 --> 45:25.750
Hello? Is this the Ueno residence?

45:25.750 --> 45:26.430
Yes?

45:26.430 --> 45:27.590
Huh?

45:27.590 --> 45:28.880
May I ask who’s calling?

45:28.880 --> 45:32.860
Err, my name is Kubo. Is, uh…

45:32.860 --> 45:36.970
Mr. Kubo? Hang on
a minute, please.

45:36.970 --> 45:39.800
Hey, Yoshiko. It’s some
guy called “Kubo.”

45:39.800 --> 45:43.870
What? Oh, no! What should I do?

45:43.870 --> 45:47.570
Hello… Hello, Kubo?

45:55.560 --> 45:58.060
So Ueno dumped you.

45:58.630 --> 46:03.660
Well, it can’t be helped. Normal
folk just don’t understand us.

46:06.420 --> 46:08.030
Hey…

46:08.600 --> 46:11.170
Are we really that depressing?!

46:11.170 --> 46:13.760
Are we really that weird?

46:13.760 --> 46:18.240
Is it a crime to love anime
or SFX movies?

46:18.240 --> 46:20.500
Why should it be a reason
to set us apart?

46:20.780 --> 46:22.780
Playing tennis is no problem…

46:22.780 --> 46:26.480
…but if you watch anime,
you’re weird? Why?!

46:26.990 --> 46:28.940
Kubo…

46:31.930 --> 46:34.060
Dammit…

46:34.060 --> 46:36.580
Dammit… Dammit…

46:36.580 --> 46:38.150
<i>Dammit</i>!

46:40.870 --> 46:45.070
Tanaka! I quit! No more
job hunting for me!

46:45.070 --> 46:46.530
Huh?!

46:47.770 --> 46:50.110
If otaku are going to be
discriminated against…

46:50.110 --> 46:51.990
…then so be it! I’m gonna
become a total otaku!

46:52.270 --> 46:53.490
Say what?!

46:53.760 --> 46:57.350
I’m gonna be not just an otaku,
but the otaku of otaku…

46:57.350 --> 46:59.620
…Otaking!

46:59.620 --> 47:02.350
“Otaking?”

47:03.940 --> 47:07.620
I like it! I like it!

47:07.620 --> 47:09.820
All right, I quit too!

47:09.820 --> 47:14.230
Let’s both become… Otakings!

47:23.940 --> 47:26.510
Oh well…

48:03.870 --> 48:14.190
July 7, 1984:
Theatrical premiere of
”Super Dimension Fortress Macross:
Do You Remember Love?”

48:34.740 --> 48:42.560
<i>In the middle of summer, around Harumi,</i>
<i>I walk around carrying the ‘zines.</i>

48:42.560 --> 48:50.060
<i>Before I know it, you fade away</i>
<i>and I can’t see you.</i>

48:50.060 --> 48:57.680
<i>You tacky, unsightly person, even an</i>
<i>eternal love would fade with you.</i>

48:57.680 --> 49:05.930
<i>I love you, but I don’t think</i>
<i>I can go your way.</i>

49:07.410 --> 49:11.440
<i>Understand it.</i>

49:11.440 --> 49:15.350
<i>No, I hate it.</i>

49:15.350 --> 49:19.230
<i>There’s nothing I can do.</i>

49:19.230 --> 49:22.830
<i>You’ve changed.</i>

49:22.830 --> 49:26.570
<i>Cosplay is my reason for living.</i>

49:26.570 --> 49:30.370
<i>I guess we can’t go back</i>
<i>to the way things were…</i>

49:30.370 --> 49:34.210
<i>I’ve finally found my life.</i>

49:34.210 --> 49:37.810
<i>You live in a different world.</i>

49:37.810 --> 49:45.390
<i>Oh, happiness that</i>
<i>can’t be matched.</i>

49:45.390 --> 49:53.490
<i>The lost way of otaku…</i>
